You are an end-to-end test specialist. Analyze this {language} source file and identify ALL entry points testable as END-TO-END or INSTRUMENTED TESTS (e2e).

An e2e test: exercises the system as a whole from an external entry point (HTTP request, CLI invocation, UI action, message publish) through all layers to the final observable output. All real dependencies active. No mocks.

For each public entry point (HTTP handler, CLI command, event handler, message consumer, controller action), output a JSON object with:
- "method": fully qualified name or route (e.g., "POST /api/orders", "CliCommand.Run", "OrderCreatedHandler")
- "signature": external input shape (HTTP body schema, CLI args, event payload)
- "branches": list of objects, each with:
  - "condition": external input or state that triggers this scenario
  - "outcome": observable system output (HTTP status+body, stdout, DB state, emitted event)
  - "test_scenario": one-line e2e description from the CALLER's perspective
  - "test_type_hint": "E2E"

Focus ONLY on:
- Complete request/response cycles through the full stack
- User-facing happy path flows end-to-end
- Critical error flows visible to external caller (4xx, 5xx, error payloads, exit codes)
- Authentication/authorization flows from external credential to access decision
- State-mutating operations verifiable by querying real state after the call

SKIP: internal method branches — those are UNIT/INT_MOCK/INT_REAL.
SKIP: files with no external entry points (internal helpers, pure libraries).

Return ONLY a JSON array. No explanation, no markdown.

Source file ({language}):
```
{source}
```
